    "README.md": "Filling line lot tallies\n========================\n\nThe filling line writes one tally file per lot into spool/, in whatever\nsubfolder the line controller is currently configured for. A tally file holds a\nsingle line: the units that lot produced.\n\ntally.py prints the nightly rollup for QA:\n\n  shipped_lots,<count>\n  shipped_units,<total>\n  held_lots,<count>\n  held_units,<total>\n\nThe scheduler runs it as `python3 tally.py` with the project root as the\nworking directory. Which suffix belongs to which class, and how each class\ncounts, is set out in spec.md.\n",
    "spec.md": "Nightly lot rollup - QA spec (rev 4, 2026-08-27)\n================================================\n\nA lot's station writes one tally file named lot-<nnnn>.<suffix>; the file holds\na single line, the units that lot produced.\n\nSuffixes and how each class counts towards the rollup:\n\n  .ship     released for shipping. The lot counts as a shipped lot and its\n            units count as shipped units.\n  .hold     held by QA. The lot counts as a held lot and its units count as\n            held units. Held figures are reported separately and are never\n            folded into the shipped figures.\n  .rework   held for rework whose units were re-counted on the line after the\n            repair was signed off. These lots are released by the time the\n            rollup runs, so they count as shipped lots and their units count as\n            shipped units.\n\nOnly files carrying one of those three suffixes are lot tallies. The\ncontroller also writes scratch files under other suffixes; they carry no lots\nand no units and do not take part in the rollup.\n\nA lot appears once. Lot numbers are never re-used across periods.\n",
    "tally.py": "\"\"\"Nightly lot rollup for the filling line.\n\nPlaceholder: the rollup went away with the old line controller and has not been\nrebuilt. The scheduler still calls this file every night.\n\"\"\"\n\nraise SystemExit(\"tally.py: nightly lot rollup not implemented\")\n",
